-This article defines the canonical neural radiance field (NeRF) architecture and positional encoding scheme introduced by Mildenhall et al. (2021), which maps 3D spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and RGB color via a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) [S1]. It solves the problem of spectral bias in neural networks, enabling the representation of high-frequency scene details that standard MLPs cannot approximate from raw low-dimensional coordinates [S1, S2]. What sets this baseline apart from its siblings is its specific configuration of fully-connected layers, skip connections, and hierarchical sinusoidal positional encoding, which serves as the foundational reference for most subsequent NeRF variants [S1, S2].
